1
00:00:00,279 --> 00:00:03,700
part 3 chapter 5.

2
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:08,319
At each stage of his imprisonment he had known, or seemed to know, whereabouts he

3
00:00:08,319 --> 00:00:10,360
was in the windowless building.

4
00:00:10,360 --> 00:00:13,180
Possibly there were slight differences in the air pressure.

5
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,599
The cells where the guards had beaten him were below ground level.

6
00:00:16,599 --> 00:00:21,099
The room where he had been interrogated by O'Brien was high up near the roof.

7
00:00:21,099 --> 00:00:25,898
This place was many meters underground, as deep down as it was possible to go.

8
00:00:25,898 --> 00:00:30,040
It was bigger than most of the cells he had been in. But he hardly noticed his

9
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:34,000
surroundings. All he noticed was that there were two small tables straight in

10
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:39,580
front of him, each covered with green beige One was only a meter or two from him, the

11
00:00:39,580 --> 00:00:41,797
other was further away, near the door.

12
00:00:41,797 --> 00:00:47,080
He was strapped upright in a chair, so tightly that he could move nothing, not even his head

13
00:00:47,080 --> 00:00:52,540
the sort of pad gripped his head from behind, forcing him to look straight in front of him.

14
00:00:52,540 --> 00:00:56,797
For a moment he was alone, then the door opened and O'Brien came in.

15
00:00:56,797 --> 00:01:00,520
'You asked me once,' said O'Brien, 'what was in Room 101.

16
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:04,957
I told you that you knew the answer already. Everyone knows it. The thing that

17
00:01:04,957 --> 00:01:08,860
is in Room 101 is the worst thing in the world.'

18
00:01:08,860 --> 00:01:13,360
The door opened again. A guard came in, carrying something made of wire, a box or

19
00:01:13,360 --> 00:01:14,919
basket of some kind.

20
00:01:14,919 --> 00:01:17,259
He set it down on the further table.

21
00:01:17,259 --> 00:01:23,378
Because of the position in which O'Brien was standing, Winston could not see what the thing was.

22
00:01:23,378 --> 00:01:28,119
'The worst thing in the world,' said O'Brien, 'varies from individual to individual.

23
00:01:28,119 --> 00:01:33,159
It may be burial alive, or death by fire, or drowning, or by impalement, or 50 other

24
00:01:33,159 --> 00:01:38,919
deaths. There are cases where it is some quite trivial thing, not even fatal.'

25
00:01:38,919 --> 00:01:42,340
He had moved a little to one side, so that Winston had a better view of the

26
00:01:42,340 --> 00:01:43,659
thing on the table.

27
00:01:43,659 --> 00:01:47,977
It was an oblong wire cage with a handle on top for carrying it by.

28
00:01:47,977 --> 00:01:51,759
Fixed to the front of it was something that looked like a fencing mask, with the

29
00:01:51,759 --> 00:01:53,680
concave side outwards.

30
00:01:53,680 --> 00:01:57,040
Although it was three or four meters away from him, he could see that the cage

31
00:01:57,040 --> 00:02:02,200
was divided lengthways into two compartments, and that there was some kind of creature in each

32
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:04,779
there were rats.

33
00:02:04,779 --> 00:02:10,360
'In your case,' said O'Brien, 'the worst thing in the world happens to be rats.'

34
00:02:10,360 --> 00:02:14,619
A sort of premonitory tremor, a fear of he was not certain what, had passed

35
00:02:14,619 --> 00:02:18,340
through Winston as soon as he caught his first glimpse of the cage.

36
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:21,457
But at this moment the meaning of the mask-like attachment in front of it

37
00:02:21,457 --> 00:02:23,200
suddenly sank into him.

38
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:25,898
His bowels seemed to turn to water.

39
00:02:25,898 --> 00:02:29,619
'You can't do that!' he cried out in a high-cracked voice. 'You couldn't, you

40
00:02:29,619 --> 00:02:32,558
couldn't! It's impossible.'

41
00:02:32,558 --> 00:02:36,099
'Do you remember,' said O'Brien, 'the moment of panic that used to occur in your

42
00:02:36,099 --> 00:02:40,240
dreams? There was a wall of blackness in front of you, and a roaring sound in your

43
00:02:40,240 --> 00:02:43,659
ears. There was something terrible on the other side of the wall.

44
00:02:43,659 --> 00:02:47,740
You knew that you knew what it was, but you dared not drag it into the open.

45
00:02:47,740 --> 00:02:51,279
It was the rats that were on the other side of the wall.'

46
00:02:51,279 --> 00:02:57,099
'O'Brien!' said Winston, making an effort to control his voice. 'You know this is not necessary.

47
00:02:57,099 --> 00:03:00,398
What is it that you want me to do?'

48
00:03:00,398 --> 00:03:04,718
O'Brien made no direct answer. When he spoke it was in the schoolmaster-ish

49
00:03:04,718 --> 00:03:06,939
manner that he sometimes affected.

50
00:03:06,939 --> 00:03:10,419
He looked thoughtfully into the distance, as though he were addressing an audience

51
00:03:10,419 --> 00:03:16,000
somewhere behind Winston's back foreign By itself,' he said, 'pain is not always

52
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,797
enough. There are occasions when a human being will stand out against pain, even

53
00:03:20,797 --> 00:03:22,659
to the point of death.

54
00:03:22,659 --> 00:03:26,439
But for everyone there is something unendurable something that cannot be

55
00:03:26,439 --> 00:03:30,939
contemplated. Courage and cowardice are not involved. If you are falling from a

56
00:03:30,939 --> 00:03:35,500
height it is not cowardly to clutch at a rope. If you have come up from deep water

57
00:03:35,500 --> 00:03:39,580
it is not cowardly to fill your lungs with air. It is merely an instinct which

58
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:43,599
cannot be destroyed It is the same with the rats. For you,

59
00:03:43,599 --> 00:03:47,319
they are unendurable. They are a form of pressure that you cannot withstand, even

60
00:03:47,319 --> 00:03:48,878
if you wished to.

61
00:03:48,878 --> 00:03:51,939
You will do what is required of you.'

62
00:03:51,939 --> 00:03:54,819
'But what is it, what is it? How can I do

63
00:03:54,819 --> 00:03:57,218
it if I don't know what it is?'

64
00:03:57,218 --> 00:04:00,878
O'Brien picked up the cage and brought it across to the nearer table.

65
00:04:00,878 --> 00:04:03,580
He set it down carefully on the beige cloth.

66
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:07,718
Winston could hear the blood singing in his ears. He had the feeling of sitting

67
00:04:07,718 --> 00:04:09,159
in utter loneliness.

68
00:04:09,159 --> 00:04:12,700
He was in the middle of a great empty plane a flat desert drenched with

69
00:04:12,700 --> 00:04:17,020
sunlight, across which all sounds came to him out of immense distances

70
00:04:17,020 --> 00:04:21,457
hit the cage with the rats was not two meters away from him. They were enormous

71
00:04:21,457 --> 00:04:26,500
rats. They were at the age when a rat's muzzle grows blunt and fierce and it's

72
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:31,718
fur brown instead of gray The rat,' said O'Brien, still addressing

73
00:04:31,718 --> 00:04:36,637
his invisible audience, 'although a rodent, is carnivorous. You are aware of that.

74
00:04:36,637 --> 00:04:40,599
You will have heard of the things that happen in the poor quarters of this town.

75
00:04:40,599 --> 00:04:45,398
In some streets a woman dare not leave her baby alone in the house, even for five minutes.

76
00:04:45,398 --> 00:04:47,977
The rats are certain to attack it.

77
00:04:47,977 --> 00:04:51,580
Within quite a small time they will strip it to the bones.

78
00:04:51,580 --> 00:04:54,218
They also attack secret dying people.

79
00:04:54,218 --> 00:04:59,319
They show astonishing intelligence in knowing when a human being is helpless.'

80
00:04:59,319 --> 00:05:02,080
There was an outburst of squeals from the cage.

81
00:05:02,080 --> 00:05:04,840
It seemed to reach Winston from far away.

82
00:05:04,840 --> 00:05:09,099
The rats were fighting; they were trying to get at each other through the partition.

83
00:05:09,099 --> 00:05:11,860
He heard also a deep groan of despair.

84
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:16,058
That, too, seemed to come from outside himself.

85
00:05:16,058 --> 00:05:20,020
O'Brien picked up the cage, and, as he did so, pressed something in it.

86
00:05:20,020 --> 00:05:21,878
There was a sharp click.

87
00:05:21,878 --> 00:05:25,898
Winston made a frantic effort to tear himself loose from the chair.

88
00:05:25,898 --> 00:05:30,279
It was hopeless, every part of him, even his head, was held immovably.

89
00:05:30,279 --> 00:05:36,099
O'Brien moved to the cage nearer. It was less than a meter from Winston's face.

90
00:05:36,099 --> 00:05:41,080
'I have pressed the first lever,' said a bran You understand the construction of this cage.

91
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:44,319
The mask will fit over your head, leaving no exit.

92
00:05:44,319 --> 00:05:47,740
When I pressed this other lever, the door of the cage will slide up.

93
00:05:47,740 --> 00:05:50,619
These starving brutes will shoot out of it like bullets.

94
00:05:50,619 --> 00:05:54,340
Have you ever seen a rat leap through the air? They will leap onto your face

95
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:58,240
and bore straight into it. Sometimes they attack the eyes first.

96
00:05:58,240 --> 00:06:02,558
Sometimes they burrow through the cheeks and devour the tongue.'

97
00:06:02,558 --> 00:06:04,659
The cage was nearer; it was closing in.

98
00:06:04,659 --> 00:06:08,500
Winston heard a succession of shrill cries which appeared to be occurring in

99
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:10,180
the air above his head.

100
00:06:10,180 --> 00:06:14,680
But he fought furiously against his panic. To think, to think, even with a

101
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:17,740
split second lift to think was the only hope.

102
00:06:17,740 --> 00:06:21,878
Suddenly the foul musty odor of the brutes struck his nostrils. There was a

103
00:06:21,878 --> 00:06:26,200
violent convulsion of nausea inside him, and he almost lost consciousness.

104
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:31,180
Everything had gone black. For an instant he was insane, a screaming animal.

105
00:06:31,180 --> 00:06:34,957
Yet he came out of the blackness clutching an idea. There was one and only

106
00:06:34,957 --> 00:06:38,860
one way to save himself. He must interpose another human being, the body

107
00:06:38,860 --> 00:06:43,000
of another human being, between himself and the rats.

108
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:47,200
The circle of the mask was large enough now to shut out the vision of anything else.

109
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:50,319
The wire door was a couple of hand spans from his face.

110
00:06:50,319 --> 00:06:52,540
The rats knew what was coming now.

111
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:57,159
One of them was leaping up and down, the other, an old scaly grandfather of the

112
00:06:57,159 --> 00:07:02,319
sewers, stood up, with his pink hands against the bars, and fiercely sniffed the air.

113
00:07:02,319 --> 00:07:06,580
Winston could see the whiskers and the yellow teeth. Again the black panic took

114
00:07:06,580 --> 00:07:10,540
hold of him. He was blind, helpless, mindless.

115
00:07:10,540 --> 00:07:16,058
'It was a common punishment in Imperial China,' said O'Brien as didactically as ever.

116
00:07:16,058 --> 00:07:20,740
The mass was closing on his face. The wire brushed his cheek.

117
00:07:20,740 --> 00:07:26,319
And then no it was not relief, only hope, a tiny fragment of hope. Too late, perhaps

118
00:07:26,319 --> 00:07:27,580
too late.

119
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:30,759
But he had suddenly understood that in the whole world there was just one

120
00:07:30,759 --> 00:07:35,137
person to whom he could transfer his punishment— one body that he could thrust between

121
00:07:35,137 --> 00:07:39,520
himself and the rats. And he was shouting frantically, over and over:

122
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:42,457
'Do it to Julia! Do it to Julia! Not me!

123
00:07:42,457 --> 00:07:45,878
Julia! I don't care what you do to her.

124
00:07:45,878 --> 00:07:48,040
Tear her face off, strip her to the bones.

125
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:52,477
Not me! Julia! Not me!'

126
00:07:52,477 --> 00:07:56,319
He was falling backwards, into enormous depths, away from the rats.

127
00:07:56,319 --> 00:08:00,040
He was still strapped in the chair, but he had fallen through the floor, through

128
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:03,218
the walls of the building, through the earth, through the oceans, through the

129
00:08:03,218 --> 00:08:08,439
atmosphere, into outer space, into the gulfs between the stars— —always away, away

130
00:08:08,439 --> 00:08:14,137
from the rats. He was light years distant, but O'Brien was still standing at his side.

131
00:08:14,137 --> 00:08:18,279
There was still the cold touch of wire against his cheek. But through the

132
00:08:18,279 --> 00:08:22,360
darkness that enveloped him he heard another metallic click, and knew that the

133
00:08:22,360 --> 00:08:25,000
cage door had clicked shut and not open
